# Heki — a persistence adapter backed by an append-log file per aggregate.
#
# The contract : it DECLARES the port it implements (the inverted arrow) ; the
# port never names it. `persisted_by("Heki")` resolves if and only if the
# persistence port carries the `persisted_by` verb — the typed attach checkpoint
# Sqlite and Memory pass through too.
#
# The third sibling on one port, and the one that shows the port is real. Memory
# keeps records in a Hash and forgets them ; Sqlite puts them in a table with a
# projected schema ; Heki writes one compressed file per aggregate. Same verb,
# same reply signal, three entirely different stories about where a balance
# lives — and the domain says `persisted_by("…")` and learns none of them.
#
# IN-PROCESS, like every persistence adapter. Persistence is the SOLE impure
# edge that is dependency-injected and called inline, because find/save is the
# one edge with a bounded lifecycle : the runtime hydrates BEFORE the sync core
# runs and persists AFTER it returns, so the call can never stall mid-logic.
# Every other port runs out-of-process for exactly the reason this one does not.
#
# `dir` is WHERE the logs are written — a directory, not a file, because heki
# keeps one store per aggregate. Its value lives per-deployment in .world.
Hecks.adapter "Heki" do
  port   "persistence"
  field  :dir
end
